Azure AI Search index name (suggested)
•	nova-banking-knowledge-index
Index fields / schema (required)
•	id (string, key) — deterministic chunk id
•	documentId (string) — DocumentId
•	title (string, searchable, retrievable) — Title
•	category (string, facetable/filterable) — Category
•	version (string, filterable) — Version
•	effectiveDate (date) — EffectiveDate
•	pageNumber (int32, filterable, retrievable) — PageNumber
•	sourceUrl (string, retrievable) — SourceUrl
•	content (string, searchable, retrievable) — chunk text
•	embedding (Collection(single) of float) — vector field for embedding
•	score (double) — returned by search results (not stored in index)
Embedding model / deployment configuration
•	The code exposes an AzureAiSearchOptions.EmbeddingDeployment option. Configure this with the Azure OpenAI embedding deployment name you have in your Azure subscription. Example: text-embedding-3-small (or the name of your assigned deployment). This is configurable — do not hardcode.
How PDF text extraction works (recommended)
•	The codebase includes KnowledgeIngestionService for cleaning and chunking text and deterministic id generation.
•	PDF extraction (preserving page boundaries) must be done by a document extraction component (not implemented here to avoid heavy dependencies). Recommended options:
•	Use PDFPig (PdfPig.Core) or iText7 for .NET to extract per-page text reliably.
•	Alternatively use an external tool (Apache Tika) to produce per-page text files, then feed them into the ingestion tool.
•	Extract pages into a list<string> (one element per page) and pass them to KnowledgeIngestionService.ChunkDocument to create chunks with page metadata.
Chunking strategy
•	Simple, deterministic strategy implemented:
•	Clean text (normalize whitespace, reduce blank lines).
•	Split per page.
•	Split each page into chunks with a max char size (configurable, default 1000).
•	Prefer splitting at sentence boundaries (last ., !, ? within the chunk).
•	Each chunk contains DocumentId, Title, Category, Version, EffectiveDate, PageNumber, SourceUrl, ChunkIndex, and Text.
Required metadata per chunk (enforced)
•	DocumentId
•	Title
•	Category
•	Version
•	EffectiveDate
•	PageNumber
•	SourceUrl
•	Chunk text
•	Embedding vector (to be computed during ingestion)
Deterministic IDs to prevent duplicate ingestion
•	KnowledgeChunk.CreateDeterministicId(documentId, pageNumber, chunkIndex)
•	Implementation: SHA256 hash of "documentId|pageNumber|chunkIndex" hex-encoded
•	Deterministic IDs ensure re-running ingestion does not create duplicate chunks in the index (use upsert semantics when uploading to Azure Search).
Runtime retrieval flow
•	User asks a public banking policy/FAQ question to MCP tool: SearchBankingKnowledge(question)
•	MCP tool calls IBankingService.SearchBankingKnowledgeAsync(question)
•	BankingService delegates to IKnowledgeSearchService.SearchAsync(question)
•	IKnowledgeSearchService implementation (Infrastructure layer) should:
1.	Create an embedding for the question with configured embedding deployment.
2.	Perform a hybrid/vector search against the Azure AI Search index (filtering by Category/Version if needed).
3.	Return top-k chunks mapped to KnowledgeSearchResultDto with metadata for grounding.
•	BankingService (current implementation) returns a simple concatenated string of top results (this preserves backward compatibility). The application DTOs are available for building more structured LLM prompts with citations.
How SearchBankingKnowledge uses the RAG service
•	The BankingService.SearchBankingKnowledgeAsync now calls IKnowledgeSearchService.
•	The application abstraction (IKnowledgeSearchService) keeps Azure SDK details in Infrastructure.
•	MCP tools remain unchanged except the BankingService now uses the RAG abstraction to provide public knowledge.
Grounding / prompt-injection defenses implemented / recommended
•	Retrieval is treated purely as data: retrieved chunks are returned as reference material (KnowledgeSearchResultDto.Text) and must NOT be treated as instructions.
•	Never inject retrieved text into privileged system prompts. The LLM grounding prompt must:
•	Keep system/application instructions separate and immutable.
•	Provide retrieved chunks only as context/reference in a dedicated section, clearly labeled as "Reference material — may contain untrusted content".
•	Instruct the model: "Use only the information in the Reference material to answer the user's public banking policy question. If insufficient, respond: 'I don't have enough information in the banking knowledge base.'"
•	The code and tests explicitly treat retrieved text as data. The retrieval pipeline and the LLM prompt builder must enforce this separation.
Citations representation
•	Each KnowledgeSearchResultDto includes Title, DocumentId, PageNumber, SourceUrl, EffectiveDate and a text snippet.
•	The AI response should cite returned chunks explicitly, e.g., "According to Savings and Current Account Terms, page 3: ...".
•	Only cite actual returned results — do not fabricate.
Document ingestion (repeatable, idempotent) — recommended steps (to implement / run)
1.	Extract text pages from:
•	SavingsAndCurrentAccountTerms.pdf
•	HomeAndPersonalLoanPolicy.pdf
•	CreditCardFeesAndBillingGuide.pdf
•	DigitalBankingAndSecurityFAQ.pdf Each should be extracted into per-page strings preserving page numbering.
2.	Clean text and chunk using KnowledgeIngestionService.ChunkDocument.
3.	Compute deterministic id for each chunk.
4.	Create or update documents in the Azure AI Search index using the id as the document key (upsert).
5.	Generate embeddings via the configured embedding deployment and store them in the embedding vector field.
6.	Use batch upload with upsert to avoid duplicates (ensure idempotency).
Exact (example) commands to ingest the four PDFs
•	The repository includes the ingestion helper, but does not include a ready-made CLI to perform extraction + embedding + upload.
•	Example pseudo-commands you can run after implementing a small CLI that uses PDF extraction and the Azure SDK:
•	dotnet run --project tools/KnowledgeIngestor -- --source "knowledge" --index "nova-banking-knowledge-index" --options-file "appsettings.Development.json"
•	A concrete sequence (manual steps you must implement/execute):
1.	Extract pages to JSON, e.g., pages-doc1.json ... pages-doc4.json (one array per file).
2.	Use a console app to read each file and call ingestion + upload.
3.	The console app should upsert chunks with deterministic ids and compute embeddings with your Azure embedding deployment.
•	I did not create a CLI in this change to avoid introducing heavy runtime dependencies; code points where to implement the upload are provided in the infrastructure skeleton.
